<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kasivisvesvara Temple, Lakkundi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kasivisvesvara Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadag">Gadag</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, built in the late Western Chalukya period, probably in the twelfth century. Dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, it is celebrated for the exceptionally fine and dense carving of its doorways and outer walls and is considered one of the finest examples of later Chalukya architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kasivisvesvara-temple-lakkundi.jpg" alt="Kasivisvesvara Temple, Lakkundi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P. Madhusudan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:11th_century_Kasivisvesvara_temple,_Lakkundi,_Karnataka_India_-_12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
